/-
# De-risk: declared arith host-role helpers pinned by TEMPLATE equality

Fourth column of the declared-envelope family (`dCtorBody` / `concatPinnedAt`
lower the TYPE section from declaration; this module lowers the arith helper
CODE bodies). The certificate DECLARES which function index carries each of
the host-role contracts — `Int.add` / `Int.sub` / `Int.mul`, the Int-carrier
`box` constructor and the `toIndex` index-extraction helper (the manifest
`hostRoleTable`) — plus the handful of indices the helper bodies mention; the
wall SYNTHESIZES the canonical helper body from that declaration
(meaning -> bytes) and pins the real code-section bytes by EQUALITY. No byte
is scanned to discover a role; a wrong declaration fails the pin.

What this equality does and does not establish is worth stating exactly,
because it is easy to overstate. It fixes WHICH CODE runs behind a role: the
function the certificate nominates carries the canonical helper body and not
some other body of the same signature. It does NOT establish what that code
COMPUTES. The arithmetic, box and index-extraction contracts remain explicit
hypotheses of `Obligation.holds`, disclosed by `ClaimAxes`; pinning bytes
narrows the artifact, not the trusted-computing base.

The helper bodies are generated by our compiler from fixed WAT templates
(`src/codegen/wasm_gc/builtins/bignum.rs`, `emit_aint_addsub` /
`emit_aint_mul` / `emit_aint_from_i64` / `emit_aint_to_index`), so the honest
body is a pure function of six declared indices:

* `carrier`   — the `$aint` struct type index (the Int carrier),
* `limb`      — the limb array type index the bignum path reads,
* `decompose` / `normalize` / `strip` / `umagCmp` — the four factored
  bignum sub-routine FUNCTION indices the helper bodies call.

Every other byte of each body is a literal fixed by the WAT template. Each
declared index is spliced through the canonical LEB128 encoding wasm uses at
that position: `call` targets and `struct.new` / `struct.get` / array-op type
indices are unsigned u32 (`CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes`); `ref.null` and
`(ref null _)` heap-type positions are SIGNED s33 (`CertPrelude.s33Bytes`),
where 64 already needs a second byte. `checkArithHostParams` bounds all six
indices below `2 ^ 32` — the whole u32 index space the wasm binary format
admits — and the fuel-bounded encoders are exact on that entire range, so no
real module's declaration can leave the encoded regime. An earlier revision
spliced each index as a single raw byte and bounded it below 128, which made
the synthesized body one byte short for any module whose helper indices need
a two-byte LEB — an unprovable acceptance pin (a false NEGATIVE) for large
honest programs.
-/

import CertPrelude

namespace ArithTemplateDerisk

/-- The arith host roles the manifest `hostRoleTable` can declare. Every one of
    them is pinned by template equality (`AcceptedArtifact.arithTableCheck`).

    `box` and `toIndex` are pinned a SECOND way as well, by their runtime export
    names, and neither pin subsumes the other. The export-name equality fixes
    WHICH INDEX the role may be declared at, and it is the only pin with any
    force when the declaration is `none` — the template equality is vacuous
    there by construction, so without the name pin an absent `toIndex` would be
    a free choice rather than a byte-proved absence. The template equality fixes
    WHICH BYTES sit at that index, which the name pin says nothing about. -/
inductive ArithRole where
  | box | add | sub | mul | toIndex
deriving Repr, DecidableEq

/-- The DECLARED indices the helper bodies are a function of. Nothing here is
    computed from bytes: the producer declares them, the wall synthesizes. -/
structure ArithHostParams where
  carrier   : Nat
  limb      : Nat
  decompose : Nat
  normalize : Nat
  strip     : Nat
  umagCmp   : Nat
deriving Repr, DecidableEq

/-- LEB regime, fail-closed: every declared index lies below `2 ^ 32`, the
    whole u32 index space the wasm binary format admits. The bound is what
    ties the templates to their encoders: `CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes` is exact
    below `2 ^ 35` and `CertPrelude.s33Bytes` below `2 ^ 41`, so inside this
    band every splice is the canonical encoding wasm itself uses, and the
    encoders' fuel-exhausted branches are unreachable. A declaration outside
    the band fails this conjunct before any template is compared. -/
def checkArithHostParams (p : ArithHostParams) : Bool :=
  decide (p.carrier < 4294967296) && decide (p.limb < 4294967296) &&
  decide (p.decompose < 4294967296) && decide (p.normalize < 4294967296) &&
  decide (p.strip < 4294967296) && decide (p.umagCmp < 4294967296)

/-! ## Canonical byte LOWERING of each declared helper body (meaning -> bytes)

Each definition mirrors one WAT template: a fixed literal skeleton with the
declared indices spliced in at the template's parameter positions. -/

/-- `__rt_aint_from_i64`: the canonical Small constructor
    (`src/codegen/wasm_gc/builtins/wat/from_i64.wat`), the smallest member of
    the family. Reading it: `00` an empty locals vector; `20 00` `local.get $n`;
    `d0 <limb>` `ref.null $mag`; `41 00` `i32.const 0`; `fb 00 <carrier>`
    `struct.new $aint`; `0b` `end`.

    Note the asymmetry between the two holes. `d0 <limb>` is a SIGNED (`s33`)
    heap-type position, lowered through `CertPrelude.s33Bytes` (one byte below
    64, two bytes from 64). The `fb 00 <carrier>` hole is an unsigned type
    index, lowered through `CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes` (one byte below 128).
    Splicing either hole as a raw byte would synthesize a body no emitter
    produces the moment the index outgrows one byte. -/
def boxTemplateBody (p : ArithHostParams) : List Nat :=
    [0x00, 0x20, 0x00, 0xd0] ++
    CertPrelude.s33Bytes p.limb ++
    [0x41, 0x00, 0xfb, 0x00] ++
    CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes p.carrier ++
    [0x0b]

/-- `__aint_to_index`: the fused vector-read index-extraction helper
    (`src/codegen/wasm_gc/builtins/wat/to_index.wat`). No loops, no calls, and
    the carrier is the only declared index it mentions — twice, both times at
    the UNSIGNED type-index position of a `struct.get` (`fb 02 <carrier>
    <field>`). Neither occurrence is an `s33` heap-type position, so both
    holes are lowered through `CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes`, never `s33Bytes`.
    `limb` does not occur at all.

    Reading it: `01 01 7e` one local group of one `i64`; then
    `20 00 / fb 02 <carrier> 01 / d1` — `ref.is_null (struct.get $aint $magf
    (local.get $a))`, "is this a Small?". `04 7f` opens `if (result i32)`; the
    `7f` is the `i32` blocktype, a VALUE type, not a type index. The then-branch
    loads `$small` into the local (`fb 02 <carrier> 00 / 21 01`), tests
    `0 <= n` (`42 00 59`) and `n < 2^31` (`42 80 80 80 80 08 53`) and joins them
    with `71` (`i32.and`), then returns either `a7` (`i32.wrap_i64`) or, in its
    else-branch, the `41 7f` (`i32.const -1`) out-of-bounds sentinel. The outer
    else returns that same sentinel for a Big. Two trailing `0b` close the inner
    and outer `if`, and the final `0b` closes the function body. -/
def toIndexTemplateBody (p : ArithHostParams) : List Nat :=
    [0x01, 0x01, 0x7e, 0x20, 0x00, 0xfb, 0x02] ++
    CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes p.carrier ++
    [0x01, 0xd1, 0x04, 0x7f, 0x20, 0x00, 0xfb, 0x02] ++
    CertPrelude.uleb32Bytes p.carrier ++
    [0x00, 0x21, 0x01, 0x20, 0x01, 0x42, 0x00, 0x59, 0x20, 0x01, 0x42, 0x80,
     0x80, 0x80, 0x80, 0x08, 0x53, 0x71, 0x04, 0x7f, 0x20, 0x01, 0xa7, 0x05,
     0x41, 0x7f, 0x0b, 0x05, 0x41, 0x7f, 0x0b, 0x0b]

